# Every sentence of RFC 9112 (rfc9112.txt, next to this file) that holds MUST,
# SHALL, REQUIRED, SHOULD or RECOMMENDED, and how shioko accounts for it.
#
#   @ <section>
#   > <the sentence>
#   = <status>      one or more; the kinds are described in tools/spec.sh,
#                   which also checks this file

@ 1.1
> The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT", "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED", "NOT RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this document are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14 [RFC2119] [RFC8174] when, and only when, they appear in all capitals, as shown here.
= info defines the requirement keywords used in the document

@ 2.2
> A recipient MUST parse an HTTP message as a sequence of octets in an encoding that is a superset of US-ASCII [USASCII].
= code parse request heads, response heads and chunk framing as octets through the byte cursor; never decode a message as a Unicode string before its lines are delimited
= test octets_pass_through_as_they_are
> A sender MUST NOT generate a bare CR (a CR character not immediately followed by LF) within any protocol elements other than the content.
= code write every start-line, field line and chunk line ourselves with CRLF line ends and no other CR: heads are never copied (SPEC 6), a head holding a bare CR is refused before anything is forwarded, chunk extensions and trailers are not re-emitted
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
= test the_response_head_is_written_by_us
= test chunk_extensions_and_trailers_are_dropped
> A recipient of such a bare CR MUST consider that element to be invalid or replace each bare CR with SP before processing the element or forwarding the message.
= code reject a request head that contains a CR not followed by LF anywhere, request-line included, with 400, and an upstream response head that does with 502
= test request_heads
= test requests_we_refuse_and_why
= test bare_cr_or_lf_is_refused
= test bare_cr_from_upstreams
= row step idle close plain fail:400 => closed close plain error:400
= row step response keep plain fail:502 => closed close plain error:502
= code treat a bare CR or LF as an error in heads, chunk-size lines and trailers: from a client 400, in an upstream response head 502, in an upstream's chunk-size line or trailer an abort (the response head is already sent, so its body is cut off unterminated and the connection closed)
= test bare_cr_or_lf_is_refused
= test malformed_chunks
= test request_heads
= test response_heads
= test bare_cr_from_upstreams
> An HTTP/1.1 user agent MUST NOT preface or follow a request with an extra CRLF.
= peer user agent: empty lines before a request-line are skipped (SPEC 6)
> If terminating the request message body with a line-ending is desired, then the user agent MUST count the terminating CRLF octets as part of the message body length.
= peer user agent: nothing to do; a length-delimited body is read to exactly Content-Length octets and whatever follows is read as the start of the next request (empty lines skipped)
> In the interest of robustness, a server that is expecting to receive and parse a request-line SHOULD ignore at least one empty line (CRLF) received prior to the request-line.
= code skip empty lines (CRLF) that precede a request-line
= test request_heads
= test requests_we_refuse_and_why
> A sender MUST NOT send whitespace between the start-line and the first header field.
= code serialise every head with the first field line starting directly after the start-line CRLF; never write a line that begins with SP or HTAB
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
= test the_response_head_is_written_by_us
= test an_error
> A recipient that receives whitespace between the start-line and the first header field MUST either reject the message as invalid or consume each whitespace-preceded line without further processing of it (i.e., ignore the entire line, along with any subsequent lines preceded by whitespace, until a properly formed header field is received or the header section is terminated).
= code reject a head in which the line after the start-line begins with SP or HTAB, like a folded header: 400 for a request, 502 for an upstream response (response heads are parsed as strictly as request heads)
= test request_heads
= test field_lines
= test upstream_responses_we_refuse
= row step idle close plain fail:400 => closed close plain error:400
= row step response keep plain fail:502 => closed close plain error:502
> When a server listening only for HTTP request messages, or processing what appears from the start-line to be an HTTP request message, receives a sequence of octets that does not match the HTTP-message grammar aside from the robustness exceptions listed above, the server SHOULD respond with a 400 (Bad Request) response and close the connection.
= row step idle close plain fail:400 => closed close plain error:400
= check Http.ErrorCloses
= test refused_never_forwarded

@ 2.3
> Intermediaries that process HTTP messages (i.e., all intermediaries other than those acting as tunnels) MUST send their own HTTP-version in forwarded messages, unless it is purposefully downgraded as a workaround for an upstream issue.
= code write HTTP/1.1 in every request-line sent upstream and in every status-line sent to the client, whatever version the other side used
= test request_intact
= test the_response_head_is_written_by_us
= test unknown_status_codes_are_relayed
> Such protocol downgrades SHOULD NOT be performed unless triggered by specific client attributes, such as when one or more of the request header fields (e.g., User-Agent) uniquely match the values sent by a client known to be in error.
= unused deliberate version downgrade: shioko always writes HTTP/1.1; nothing to do

@ 3
> A server that receives a method longer than any that it implements SHOULD respond with a 501 (Not Implemented) status code.
= code answer 501 to a request whose method is longer than 32 characters, once its head has been read and found to have a usable Host (a request line with such a method is not refused while it is read)
= test request_lines
= test requests_we_refuse_and_why
> A server that receives a request-target longer than any URI it wishes to parse MUST respond with a 414 (URI Too Long) status code (see Section 15.5.15 of [HTTP]).
= row step idle close plain fail:414 => closed close plain error:414
= code answer 414 and close when the request line is longer than 8 KB
= test request_head_limits
= test requests_we_refuse_and_why
> It is RECOMMENDED that all HTTP senders and recipients support, at a minimum, request-line lengths of 8000 octets.
= code accept a request line of up to 8 KB (not less than 8000 octets) and write it upstream unshortened
= test request_head_limits
= test a_long_target_is_forwarded_whole

@ 3.2
> Recipients of an invalid request-line SHOULD respond with either a 400 (Bad Request) error or a 301 (Moved Permanently) redirect with the request-target properly encoded.
= code reject with 400 a request line that is not exactly method SP request-target SP HTTP-version CRLF, whose target is not in origin form (starting with "/"), or that holds a bare CR or LF
= test request_lines
= test request_heads
= test requests_we_refuse_and_why
= row step idle close plain fail:400 => closed close plain error:400
= code answer 400 to a request line whose target is not origin form or holds anything but visible ASCII characters
= test request_lines
= test request_line_is_sound
= deviation SPEC 6, known departures: a target may hold visible ASCII characters the URI grammar forbids (| ^ [ ] { } ` " < > \) because browsers send them unencoded; such a request line is accepted and its target forwarded as received, only the redirect location encodes them
> A recipient SHOULD NOT attempt to autocorrect and then process the request without a redirect, since the invalid request-line might be deliberately crafted to bypass security filters along the request chain.
= code never repair a request line: no whitespace-tolerant splitting, no trimming, no re-encoding of the target; write the target octets upstream as received or reject with 400
= test request_lines
= test request_line_is_sound
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
> A client MUST send a Host header field (Section 7.2 of [HTTP]) in all HTTP/1.1 request messages.
= code write exactly one Host field line in every upstream request head
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
= test request_intact
= peer client: a request without Host gets 400 and the connection is closed
> If the target URI includes an authority component, then a client MUST send a field value for Host that is identical to that authority component, excluding any userinfo subcomponent and its "@" delimiter (Section 4.2 of [HTTP]).
= code write Host upstream exactly as the client sent it; never substitute the upstream address
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
= peer client: a Host that does not name the SNI site gets 421; on port 80 an unknown Host gets 404
> If the authority component is missing or undefined for the target URI, then a client MUST send a Host header field with an empty field value.
= peer client: an empty Host is neither `name` nor `name:port`, so it is malformed and gets 400 (SPEC 6)
> A server MUST respond with a 400 (Bad Request) status code to any HTTP/1.1 request message that lacks a Host header field and to any request message that contains more than one Host header field line or a Host header field with an invalid field value.
= row step idle close plain fail:400 => closed close plain error:400
= code answer 400 and close when a request head, once read, has no Host field line, more than one, or one whose value is not a non-empty host (reg-name, IPv4 address or bracketed IP literal) with an optional, possibly empty, port; this comes before any 501
= test requests_we_refuse_and_why
= test hosts

@ 3.2.1
> When making a request directly to an origin server, other than a CONNECT or server-wide OPTIONS request (as detailed below), a client MUST send only the absolute path and query components of the target URI as the request-target.
= code accept only a request-target in origin form and write it upstream as received, so the upstream only ever gets origin-form
= test request_lines
= test requests_we_refuse_and_why
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
= row step idle close plain fail:400 => closed close plain error:400
> If the target URI's path component is empty, the client MUST send "/" as the path within the origin-form of request-target.
= code write the received origin-form target unchanged; it always starts with "/" and shioko never derives a target from a URI
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
= test request_line_is_sound

@ 3.2.2
> When making a request to a proxy, other than a CONNECT or server-wide OPTIONS request (as detailed below), a client MUST send the target URI in "absolute-form" as the request-target.
= peer client of a forward proxy: shioko is not a forward proxy and never sends to one; an absolute-form target it receives is answered with 400 (documented departure, see "A server MUST accept the absolute-form" below)
> A client MUST send a Host header field in an HTTP/1.1 request even if the request-target is in the absolute-form, since this allows the Host information to be forwarded through ancient HTTP/1.0 proxies that might not have implemented Host.
= peer client: a request without Host gets 400 whatever the form of its target
> When a proxy receives a request with an absolute-form of request-target, the proxy MUST ignore the received Host header field (if any) and instead replace it with the host information of the request-target.
= peer forward proxy: not shioko's role; shioko answers an absolute-form target with 400
> A proxy that forwards such a request MUST generate a new Host field value based on the received request-target rather than forward the received Host field value.
= peer forward proxy: not shioko's role; shioko answers an absolute-form target with 400
> When an origin server receives a request with an absolute-form of request-target, the origin server MUST ignore the received Host header field (if any) and instead use the host information of the request-target.
= deviation absolute-form targets are refused with 400 (SPEC 6, known departures: "accepting them means deciding between two host names in one request"), so the Host field is never weighed against a target's host
= row step idle close plain fail:400 => closed close plain error:400
> A server MUST accept the absolute-form in requests even though most HTTP/1.1 clients will only send the absolute-form to a proxy.
= deviation SPEC 6, known departures: absolute-form request targets are answered with 400, although a server must accept them
= row step idle close plain fail:400 => closed close plain error:400

@ 3.2.3
> When making a CONNECT request to establish a tunnel through one or more proxies, a client MUST send only the host and port of the tunnel destination as the request-target.
= peer client: CONNECT is meant for forward proxies; shioko answers any CONNECT request with 400 and closes (SPEC 6)

@ 3.2.4
> When a client wishes to request OPTIONS for the server as a whole, as opposed to a specific named resource of that server, the client MUST send only "*" (%x2A) as the request-target.
= peer client: nothing to do; "*" is refused with 400 even with OPTIONS, as nginx refuses it (SPEC 6, known departures)
> If a proxy receives an OPTIONS request with an absolute-form of request-target in which the URI has an empty path and no query component, then the last proxy on the request chain MUST send a request-target of "*" when it forwards the request to the indicated origin server.
= peer forward proxy: shioko is not a proxy on an absolute-form chain; it answers an absolute-form target with 400

@ 4
> A client SHOULD ignore the reason-phrase content because it is not a reliable channel for information (it might be translated for a given locale, overwritten by intermediaries, or discarded when the message is forwarded via other versions of HTTP).
= code classify upstream responses by the three-digit status code only (1xx, 101, 204, 304, other; outside 100 to 599 is 502); never interpret the reason-phrase
= test status_lines
= test upstream_responses_we_refuse
= test response_intact
= test unknown_status_codes_are_relayed
> A server MUST send the space that separates the status-code from the reason-phrase even when the reason-phrase is absent (i.e., the status-line would end with the space).
= code write every status-line ourselves, own responses and forwarded ones alike, as HTTP/1.1 SP status-code SP reason-phrase, with the SP after the status code present even when the reason-phrase is empty
= test an_empty_reason_phrase_keeps_its_space

@ 5.1
> A server MUST reject, with a response status code of 400 (Bad Request), any received request message that contains whitespace between a header field name and colon.
= row step idle close plain fail:400 => closed close plain error:400
= code reject with 400 any request field line with SP or HTAB between the field name and the colon
= test field_lines
= test requests_we_refuse_and_why
= test refused_never_forwarded
> A proxy MUST remove any such whitespace from a response message before forwarding the message downstream.
= peer forward proxy: the sentence names proxies only; shioko as a gateway parses the upstream's response head as strictly as a request head, answers 502 to whitespace before the colon and rewrites every head it does forward, so such whitespace never reaches the client
= row step response keep plain fail:502 => closed close plain error:502

@ 5.2
> A sender MUST NOT generate a message that includes line folding (i.e., that has any field line value that contains a match to the obs-fold rule) unless the message is intended for packaging within the "message/http" media type.
= code never write a field line value that contains CRLF followed by SP or HTAB; a head with folding is refused (400 or 502) before anything is forwarded
= test field_line_is_sound
= test request_heads
= test requests_we_refuse_and_why
= test upstream_responses_we_refuse
> A server that receives an obs-fold in a request message that is not within a "message/http" container MUST either reject the message by sending a 400 (Bad Request), preferably with a representation explaining that obsolete line folding is unacceptable, or replace each received obs-fold with one or more SP octets prior to interpreting the field value or forwarding the message downstream.
= row step idle close plain fail:400 => closed close plain error:400
= code reject with 400 a request head that contains a line starting with SP or HTAB after a field line
= test field_lines
= test requests_we_refuse_and_why
> A proxy or gateway that receives an obs-fold in a response message that is not within a "message/http" container MUST either discard the message and replace it with a 502 (Bad Gateway) response, preferably with a representation explaining that unacceptable line folding was received, or replace each received obs-fold with one or more SP octets prior to interpreting the field value or forwarding the message downstream.
= row step response keep plain fail:502 => closed close plain error:502
= code answer 502 to an upstream response head that contains a folded header line, and forward nothing of that response
= test upstream_responses_we_refuse
> A user agent that receives an obs-fold in a response message that is not within a "message/http" container MUST replace each received obs-fold with one or more SP octets prior to interpreting the field value.
= peer user agent: nothing to do

@ 6.1
> A recipient MUST be able to parse the chunked transfer coding (Section 7.1) because it plays a crucial role in framing messages when the content size is not known in advance.
= row request_framing 1.1 none chunked => chunked
= row response_framing 1.1 body none chunked => chunked
= code decode chunked request and response bodies in the chunked codec (copy_chunked in http/body.rs, with chunk_size and read_fields from http/parse.rs)
= test chunks_are_decoded_and_the_rest_left_alone
= test request_intact
= test response_intact
> A sender MUST NOT apply the chunked transfer coding more than once to a message body (i.e., chunking an already chunked message is not allowed).
= row request_framing 1.1 none other => unsupported
= row response_framing 1.1 body none other => reject
= code write "Transfer-Encoding: chunked" at most once in a head and chunk a body exactly once; a received "chunked, chunked" is not exactly "chunked" and is never forwarded (400 for a request, 502 for a response)
= test request_intact
= test chunk_extensions_and_trailers_are_dropped
= test transfer_encoding_classes
= test requests_we_refuse_and_why
> If any transfer coding other than chunked is applied to a request's content, the sender MUST apply chunked as the final transfer coding to ensure that the message is properly framed.
= unused transfer codings other than chunked: shioko never applies one; a request that names one is answered with 501 and closed
= row request_framing 1.1 none other => unsupported
> If any transfer coding other than chunked is applied to a response's content, the sender MUST either apply chunked as the final transfer coding or terminate the message by closing the connection.
= unused transfer codings other than chunked: shioko never applies one; an upstream response that names one is answered with 502
= row response_framing 1.1 body none other => reject
> A server MUST NOT send a Transfer-Encoding header field in any response with a status code of 1xx (Informational) or 204 (No Content).
= row response_framing 1.1 bodiless none chunked => empty
= row client_framing empty 1.1 => empty
= code never write Transfer-Encoding in a 1xx or 204 head sent to the client: every response head, interim ones included, is written by us without the upstream's hop-by-hop fields, and our own 100 Continue carries none
= test responses_without_a_body
= test interim_and_bodiless_responses_carry_no_framing
> A server MUST NOT send a Transfer-Encoding header field in any 2xx (Successful) response to a CONNECT request (Section 9.3.6 of [HTTP]).
= unused CONNECT: every CONNECT request is answered with 400 and closed, so no 2xx response to CONNECT is ever sent
= row step idle close plain fail:400 => closed close plain error:400
> A server that receives a request message with a transfer coding it does not understand SHOULD respond with 501 (Not Implemented).
= row request_framing 1.1 none other => unsupported
= row step idle close plain fail:501 => closed close plain error:501
= code classify a well-formed Transfer-Encoding that names other codings, with or without parameters, before a final bare "chunked" as other and answer 501, after the Host check; one whose last coding is not a bare "chunked" is bad and gets 400 (Section 6.3)
= test transfer_encoding_classes
= test requests_we_refuse_and_why
> A client MUST NOT send a request containing Transfer-Encoding unless it knows the server will handle HTTP/1.1 requests (or later minor revisions); such knowledge might be in the form of specific user configuration or by remembering the version of a prior received response.
= peer client: a Transfer-Encoding in an HTTP/1.0 request is rejected with 400 and the connection is closed
= row request_framing 1.0 none chunked => reject
= deviation the upstream is required to speak HTTP/1.1; that is a requirement on the configuration (SPEC 3 and 6), not something shioko finds out
> A server MUST NOT send a response containing Transfer- Encoding unless the corresponding request indicates HTTP/1.1 (or later minor revisions).
= row client_framing chunked 1.0 => close
= row client_framing close 1.0 => close
= check Http.NoChunkedInHttp10
> Regardless, the server MUST close the connection after responding to such a request to avoid the potential attacks.
= row request_framing 1.1 one chunked => reject
= row step idle close plain fail:400 => closed close plain error:400
= check Http.AmbiguousRejected
= check Http.ErrorCloses
> A server or client that receives an HTTP/1.0 message containing a Transfer-Encoding header field MUST treat the message as if the framing is faulty, even if a Content-Length is present, and close the connection after processing the message.
= row request_framing 1.0 none chunked => reject
= row request_framing 1.0 one chunked => reject
= row request_framing 1.0 none other => reject
= row response_framing 1.0 body none chunked => reject
= row response_framing 1.0 body one chunked => reject
= row response_framing 1.0 bodiless none chunked => reject
= row step idle close plain fail:400 => closed close plain error:400
= row step response keep plain fail:502 => closed close plain error:502
= check Http.NoChunkedInHttp10
= code close the upstream connection after a rejected response; never read from it again
= test an_upstream_is_closed_when_its_exchange_ends

@ 6.2
> A sender MUST NOT send a Content-Length header field in any message that contains a Transfer-Encoding header field.
= row request_framing 1.1 one chunked => reject
= row response_framing 1.1 body one chunked => reject
= check Http.AmbiguousRejected
= code write at most one of Content-Length and Transfer-Encoding in any head shioko sends: the client's own are removed and ours is the body length or "Transfer-Encoding: chunked", chosen by client_framing for responses
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
= test request_intact
= test the_response_head_is_written_by_us
= test interim_and_bodiless_responses_carry_no_framing

@ 6.3
> A client MUST ignore any Content-Length or Transfer-Encoding header fields received in such a message.
= unused CONNECT: shioko never sends a CONNECT request upstream; one from a client is answered with 400
= row step idle close plain fail:400 => closed close plain error:400
> An intermediary that chooses to forward the message MUST first remove the received Content-Length field and process the Transfer-Encoding (as described below) prior to forwarding the message downstream.
= row request_framing 1.1 one chunked => reject
= row response_framing 1.1 body one chunked => reject
= check Http.AmbiguousRejected
= test refused_never_forwarded
> If a Transfer-Encoding header field is present in a request and the chunked transfer coding is not the final encoding, the message body length cannot be determined reliably; the server MUST respond with the 400 (Bad Request) status code and then close the connection.
= code class a Transfer-Encoding that does not end in "chunked" as bad (400), and one that ends in "chunked" after other codings as other (501)
= test transfer_encoding_classes
= test requests_we_refuse_and_why
= row request_framing 1.1 none bad => reject
= row request_framing 1.1 none other => unsupported
= row step idle close plain fail:501 => closed close plain error:501
= check Http.ErrorCloses
> If a message is received without Transfer-Encoding and with an invalid Content-Length header field, then the message framing is invalid and the recipient MUST treat it as an unrecoverable error, unless the field value can be successfully parsed as a comma-separated list (Section 5.6.1 of [HTTP]), all values in the list are valid, and all values in the list are the same (in which case, the message is processed with that single value used as the Content-Length field value).
= row request_framing 1.1 bad none => reject
= row request_framing 1.0 bad none => reject
= row response_framing 1.1 body bad none => reject
= row response_framing 1.1 bodiless bad none => reject
= code classify Content-Length as bad unless it is one field line of 1*DIGIT that fits the integer type; repeated lines and comma lists, even of equal values, are bad (RFC 9110 8.6 allows rejecting them)
= test content_length_classes
= test requests_we_refuse_and_why
= test upstream_responses_we_refuse
> If the unrecoverable error is in a request message, the server MUST respond with a 400 (Bad Request) status code and then close the connection.
= row request_framing 1.1 bad none => reject
= row step idle close plain fail:400 => closed close plain error:400
= check Http.ErrorCloses
> If it is in a response message received by a proxy, the proxy MUST close the connection to the server, discard the received response, and send a 502 (Bad Gateway) response to the client.
= row response_framing 1.1 body bad none => reject
= row step response keep plain fail:502 => closed close plain error:502
= code after a rejected upstream response close the upstream connection and forward none of its octets
= test upstream_responses_we_refuse
= test an_upstream_is_closed_when_its_exchange_ends
> If it is in a response message received by a user agent, the user agent MUST close the connection to the server and discard the received response.
= peer user agent: nothing to do
> If the sender closes the connection or the recipient times out before the indicated number of octets are received, the recipient MUST consider the message to be incomplete and close the connection.
= row step forward keep plain gone:- => closed close plain
= row step relay keep plain fail:502 => closed close plain
= test next_request_at_boundary
= code when fewer octets than Content-Length arrive before the sender closes or the read times out, treat the message as incomplete: stop forwarding, never pad or terminate the framing on the other side, and close both connections
= test upstreams_are_always_closed
= test end_only_when_whole
= test request_intact
= test a_length_is_copied_exactly_and_the_rest_left_alone
> Since there is no way to distinguish a successfully completed, close-delimited response message from a partially received message interrupted by network failure, a server SHOULD generate encoding or length-delimited messages whenever possible.
= row client_framing close 1.1 => chunked
= row client_framing length 1.1 => length
= code send every own response (errors and the 301) with a Content-Length
= test an_error
= test a_redirect_and_a_token
= test an_error_answer_to_head_has_no_body
> Unless a transfer coding other than chunked has been applied, a client that sends a request containing a message body SHOULD use a valid Content-Length header field if the message body length is known in advance, rather than the chunked transfer coding, since some existing services respond to chunked with a 411 (Length Required) status code even though they understand the chunked transfer coding.
= row request_framing 1.1 one none => length
= code forward a request that arrived with Content-Length using the same Content-Length; never re-chunk it
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
= test request_intact
> A user agent that sends a request that contains a message body MUST send either a valid Content-Length header field or use the chunked transfer coding.
= peer user agent: without either field the request has no body (request_framing 1.1 none none => empty) and the octets that follow are read as the next request head, which normally ends in 400 and a close
> A client MUST NOT use the chunked transfer coding unless it knows the server will handle HTTP/1.1 (or later) requests; such knowledge can be in the form of specific user configuration or by remembering the version of a prior received response.
= row request_framing 1.0 none chunked => reject
= deviation the upstream is required to speak HTTP/1.1; that is a requirement on the configuration (SPEC 3 and 6), not something shioko finds out
> A client MUST NOT process, cache, or forward such extra data as a separate response, since such behavior would be vulnerable to cache poisoning.
= test one_final_response
= test response_intact
= code once the final upstream response is complete, close the upstream connection and discard any further octets from it
= test response_intact
= test request_intact
= test an_upstream_is_closed_when_its_exchange_ends

@ 7.1
> A recipient MUST be able to parse and decode the chunked transfer coding.
= row request_framing 1.1 none chunked => chunked
= row response_framing 1.1 body none chunked => chunked
= code decode chunk-size lines, chunk data, the last chunk and the trailer section in the chunked codec
= test chunks_are_decoded_and_the_rest_left_alone
= test malformed_chunks
= test chunk_sizes
> Therefore, recipients MUST anticipate potentially large hexadecimal numerals and prevent parsing errors due to integer conversion overflows or precision loss due to integer representation.
= code parse chunk-size (and Content-Length) with checked arithmetic into a 64-bit integer and treat overflow as a framing error (400 for a request, abort for a response); never wrap, truncate or panic
= test chunk_sizes
= test chunk_size_is_sound
= test content_length_classes
= test a_chunked_body_over_the_limit_is_refused_before_it_is_copied
> Their presence SHOULD be treated as an error.
= row request_framing 1.1 none other => unsupported
= row request_framing 1.1 none bad => reject
= row response_framing 1.1 body none other => reject
= row response_framing 1.1 body none bad => reject
= code classify "chunked" followed by any parameter as not exactly "chunked" (other or bad), never as chunked
= test transfer_encoding_classes
= test requests_we_refuse_and_why

@ 7.1.1
> A recipient MUST ignore unrecognized chunk extensions.
= code accept and skip chunk extensions on every chunk-size line, in both directions, and never re-emit them
= test chunk_sizes
= test chunks_are_decoded_and_the_rest_left_alone
= test chunk_extensions_and_trailers_are_dropped

@ 7.1.2
> A recipient that retains a received trailer field MUST either store/ forward the trailer field separately from the received header fields or merge the received trailer field into the header section.
= code read the trailer section of a chunked request or response to its end and drop every trailer field; none is retained, forwarded or stored
= test chunks_are_decoded_and_the_rest_left_alone
= test chunk_extensions_and_trailers_are_dropped
> A recipient MUST NOT merge a received trailer field into the header section unless its corresponding header field definition explicitly permits and instructs how the trailer field value can be safely merged.
= code never merge a trailer field into the header section: trailers are read and dropped
= test chunk_extensions_and_trailers_are_dropped

@ 7.2
> The presence of parameters with any of these compression codings SHOULD be treated as an error.
= unused compression transfer codings: any well-formed Transfer-Encoding other than exactly "chunked", with or without parameters, is refused (501 for a request, 502 for a response)
= row request_framing 1.1 none other => unsupported
= row response_framing 1.1 body none other => reject

@ 7.3
> Registrations MUST include the following fields:
= info instruction for registrations in the IANA transfer coding registry
> Names of transfer codings MUST NOT overlap with names of content codings (Section 8.4.1 of [HTTP]) unless the encoding transformation is identical, as is the case for the compression codings defined in Section 7.2.
= info requirement on names registered as transfer codings
> Future registrations of transfer codings SHOULD NOT define parameters called "q" (case-insensitively) in order to avoid ambiguities.
= info requirement on future registrations
> Values to be added to this namespace require IETF Review (see Section 4.8 of [RFC8126]) and MUST conform to the purpose of transfer coding defined in this specification.
= info IANA registration policy

@ 7.4
> A client MUST NOT send the chunked transfer coding name in TE; chunked is always acceptable for HTTP/1.1 recipients.
= code never write a TE field in an upstream request head (TE is on SPEC 6's hop-by-hop list)
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
= peer client: a TE field that names chunked is removed with the other hop-by-hop fields; nothing else to do
> Since the TE header field only applies to the immediate connection, a sender of TE MUST also send a "TE" connection option within the Connection header field (Section 7.6.1 of [HTTP]) in order to prevent the TE header field from being forwarded by intermediaries that do not support its semantics.
= unused TE negotiation: shioko never sends TE; a client's TE field is removed as hop-by-hop whether or not Connection lists it

@ 8
> A client that receives an incomplete response message, which can occur when a connection is closed prematurely or when decoding a supposedly chunked transfer coding fails, MUST record the message as incomplete.
= row step response keep plain fail:502 => closed close plain error:502
= row step relay keep plain fail:502 => closed close plain
= test end_only_when_whole
= code when the upstream closes early or its chunked coding is malformed, never write the final zero chunk or pad to Content-Length; close the client connection so the client sees an incomplete message
= test a_cut_chunked_body_is_never_ended
= test end_only_when_whole
= test a_source_that_stalls_is_not_taken_for_one_that_ended
= test bare_cr_from_upstreams

@ 9.2
> A client that has more than one outstanding request on a connection MUST maintain a list of outstanding requests in the order sent and MUST associate each received response message on that connection to the first outstanding request that has not yet received a final (non- 1xx) response.
= unused pipelining towards the upstream: one request per upstream connection, so never more than one outstanding request
> If a client receives data on a connection that doesn't have outstanding requests, the client MUST NOT consider that data to be a valid response; the client SHOULD close the connection, since message delimitation is now ambiguous, unless the data consists only of one or more CRLF (which can be discarded per Section 2.2).
= test response_intact
= code read from an upstream connection only the response(s) to the single request sent on it; close it after the final response and discard anything further
= test response_intact
= test request_intact
= test an_upstream_is_closed_when_its_exchange_ends

@ 9.3
> HTTP implementations SHOULD support persistent connections.
= row step relay keep plain done:- => idle close plain read_head:-
= deviation towards the upstream there are no persistent connections: SPEC 2 drops upstream keepalive and SPEC 6 opens one connection per request "so one request cannot contaminate another"
> A client that does not support persistent connections MUST send the "close" connection option in every request message.
= code write "Connection: close" in every upstream request head that is not an upgrade request
= test request_intact
= test the_request_head_is_written_by_us
= test what_is_not_a_websocket_upgrade_is_not_forwarded_as_one
= deviation SPEC 6, known departures: an upgrade request goes upstream with "Connection: upgrade" and without "close", for the sake of WebSocket servers; the connection is closed after any answer other than 101 all the same
> A server that does not support persistent connections MUST send the "close" connection option in every response message that does not have a 1xx (Informational) status code.
= row step response close plain final:delimited => relay close plain respond:close
= row step response keep plain final:until_close => relay close plain respond:close
= code write "Connection: close" in every own response (errors and the 301); shioko does support persistent client connections otherwise
= test an_error
= test requests_we_refuse_and_why
= test close_honoured
> A server MUST read the entire request message body or close the connection after sending its response; otherwise, the remaining data on a persistent connection would be misinterpreted as the next request.
= test next_request_at_boundary
= row step forward keep plain sent:- => response keep plain read_response:-
= row step forward keep plain fail:413 => closed close plain error:413
> Likewise, a client MUST read the entire response message body if it intends to reuse the same connection for a subsequent request.
= unused upstream connection reuse: every upstream connection is closed after one exchange
> A proxy server MUST NOT maintain a persistent connection with an HTTP/1.0 client (see Appendix C.2.2 for information and discussion of the problems with the Keep-Alive header field implemented by many HTTP/1.0 clients).
= test close_honoured
= code report request:close for every HTTP/1.0 request, ignoring any "Connection: keep-alive" (Http.tla Verdict: keep only if the version is 1.1)
= test close_honoured
= test http10_keep_alive_is_not_kept
= row step idle close plain request:close => connect close plain connect:-
= row step response close plain final:delimited => relay close plain respond:close
= row step relay close plain done:- => closed close plain

@ 9.3.2
> A server MAY process a sequence of pipelined requests in parallel if they all have safe methods (Section 9.2.1 of [HTTP]), but it MUST send the corresponding responses in the same order that the requests were received.
= test responses_in_order
= test one_final_response
= test next_request_at_boundary
> A client that pipelines requests SHOULD retry unanswered requests if the connection closes before it receives all of the corresponding responses.
= unused pipelining towards the upstream: never done; an unanswered request is not retried, the client gets 502 or 504
> When retrying pipelined requests after a failed connection (a connection not explicitly closed by the server in its last complete response), a client MUST NOT pipeline immediately after connection establishment, since the first remaining request in the prior pipeline might have caused an error response that can be lost again if multiple requests are sent on a prematurely closed connection (see the TCP reset problem described in Section 9.6).
= unused pipelining towards the upstream: never done, and nothing is retried
> A user agent SHOULD NOT pipeline requests after a non-idempotent method, until the final response status code for that method has been received, unless the user agent has a means to detect and recover from partial failure conditions involving the pipelined sequence.
= peer user agent: nothing to do; pipelined requests are read one at a time, each only after the previous response is complete (Http.NextRequestAtBoundary)
> If the inbound connection fails before receiving a response, the pipelining intermediary MAY attempt to retry a sequence of requests that have yet to receive a response if the requests all have idempotent methods; otherwise, the pipelining intermediary SHOULD forward any received responses and then close the corresponding outbound connection(s) so that the outbound user agent(s) can recover accordingly.
= unused pipelining towards the upstream: never done; when the upstream connection fails before a response the client gets 502 and its connection is closed
= row step response keep plain fail:502 => closed close plain error:502

@ 9.5
> A client or server that wishes to time out SHOULD issue a graceful close on the connection.
= row step open whole even strict close:- => closed whole even strict alert:close_notify
= code on every deadline (head read, keep-alive idle, tunnel idle) end the connection in order: close_notify over TLS, then shut the socket down
= test bug_close_notify_was_missing_on_shutdown
= test the_handshake_has_a_deadline
> Implementations SHOULD constantly monitor open connections for a received closure signal and respond to it as appropriate, since prompt closure of both sides of a connection enables allocated system resources to be reclaimed.
= row step idle keep plain gone:- => closed close plain
= row step relay keep plain gone:- => closed close plain
= deviation SPEC 6, known departures: the client connection is not watched for closing while we wait for the upstream; a closure is noticed at the next client read or write
> A server SHOULD sustain persistent connections, when possible, and allow the underlying transport's flow-control mechanisms to resolve temporary overloads rather than terminate connections with the expectation that clients will retry.
= row step relay keep plain done:- => idle close plain read_head:-
= check Server.CapRespected
= code when all slots are taken, stop calling accept and let the kernel queue fill instead of closing established connections
= test no_more_connections_are_served_at_once_than_the_cap_allows
> A client sending a message body SHOULD monitor the network connection for an error response while it is transmitting the request.
= deviation SPEC 6, known departures: the upstream is not watched for an early error response while a request body is being sent; the response is read only after the whole request has been sent (check Http.ResponseAfterRequest)
> If the client sees a response that indicates the server does not wish to receive the message body and is closing the connection, the client SHOULD immediately cease transmitting the body and close its side of the connection.
= deviation SPEC 6, known departures: an early upstream response is never seen, so the body is sent to its end or until a write fails, and a failed write gives the client 502
= row step forward keep plain fail:502 => closed close plain error:502

@ 9.6
> A sender SHOULD send a Connection header field (Section 7.6.1 of [HTTP]) containing the "close" connection option when it intends to close a connection.
= row step response close plain final:delimited => relay close plain respond:close
= row step response keep plain final:until_close => relay close plain respond:close
= code write "Connection: close" in every own response and in every upstream request that is not an upgrade request
= test an_error
= test requests_we_refuse_and_why
= test request_intact
= deviation SPEC 6, known departures: an upgrade request goes upstream without "close" although the connection is closed after any answer other than 101
> A client that sends a "close" connection option MUST NOT send further requests on that connection (after the one containing the "close") and MUST close the connection after reading the final response message corresponding to this request.
= code send exactly one request on an upstream connection and close it once the final response is complete or the tunnel ends
= test request_intact
= test tunnel_rules
= test an_upstream_is_closed_when_its_exchange_ends
> A server that receives a "close" connection option MUST initiate closure of the connection (see below) after it sends the final response to the request that contained the "close" connection option.
= row step idle close plain request:close => connect close plain connect:-
= row step response close plain final:delimited => relay close plain respond:close
= row step relay close plain done:- => closed close plain
= test close_honoured
> The server SHOULD send a "close" connection option in its final response on that connection.
= row step response close plain final:delimited => relay close plain respond:close
> The server MUST NOT process any further requests received on that connection.
= test close_honoured
= row step relay close plain done:- => closed close plain
> A server that sends a "close" connection option MUST initiate closure of the connection (see below) after it sends the response containing the "close" connection option.
= row step response keep plain final:until_close => relay close plain respond:close
= row step relay close plain done:- => closed close plain
= check Http.ErrorCloses
> The server MUST NOT process any further requests received on that connection.
= test close_honoured
> A client that receives a "close" connection option MUST cease sending requests on that connection and close the connection after reading the response message containing the "close" connection option; if additional pipelined requests had been sent on the connection, the client SHOULD NOT assume that they will be processed by the server.
= code close the upstream connection after every final response, whatever its Connection field says; never send a second request on it
= test an_upstream_is_closed_when_its_exchange_ends

@ 9.7
> All HTTP data MUST be sent as TLS "application data" but is otherwise treated like a normal connection for HTTP (including potential reuse as a persistent connection).
= check Handshake.DataOnlyAfterFinished
= check Handshake.DataOnlyWhenOpen
= check Handshake.RightKeys
= row step open whole even strict write:- => open whole even strict send:data

@ 9.8
> When encountering an incomplete close, a client SHOULD treat as completed all requests for which it has received either
= unused HTTPS upstreams: shioko is never a TLS client; the upstream hop is plain HTTP
> A client detecting an incomplete close SHOULD recover gracefully.
= unused HTTPS upstreams: shioko is never a TLS client; the upstream hop is plain HTTP
> Clients MUST send a closure alert before closing the connection.
= peer client: an end of stream without close_notify is the TLS close event: shioko sends its own close_notify and closes (step open whole even strict close:- => closed whole even strict alert:close_notify) and the HTTP side sees the client as gone; request bodies are delimited by length or chunks, so a truncated one is never completed towards the upstream
> Servers SHOULD be prepared to receive an incomplete close from the client, since the client can often locate the end of server data.
= row step open whole even strict close:- => closed whole even strict alert:close_notify
= row step open whole even strict lost:- => closed whole even strict
= row step idle keep plain gone:- => closed close plain
= row step relay keep plain gone:- => closed close plain
> Servers MUST attempt to initiate an exchange of closure alerts with the client before closing the connection.
= row step open whole even strict close:- => closed whole even strict alert:close_notify
= row step open whole even strict protected:close_notify => closed whole even strict alert:close_notify
= code feed the TLS close event whenever we end a connection that can still be written to (application done, any deadline, end of the client's stream), before the socket is shut down; only a failed transport ends silently
